# Is attention a scarce resource?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is rational inattention? Why do people ignore information that would help them?"
next: {"title":"Why do people follow the crowd?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/101-why-do-people-follow-the-crowd.cast"}

## The first digit
domain:
    x:
      - 48
      - 86
    'y':
      - 0
      - 10
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 170
      w: 860
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: lps
      title: Heuristic Thinking and Limited Attention in the Car Market
      authors: Lacetera, N., Pope, D. G. & Sydnor, J. R.
      year: 2012
      finding: 'In over 22 million US wholesale used-car auctions, sale prices drop discontinuously at each 10,000-mile odometer threshold (roughly $150–$200), consistent with left-digit bias and partial inattention by final buyers. American Economic Review 102(5): 2206–36.'
    - id: simon
      title: Designing Organizations for an Information-Rich World
      authors: Simon, H. A.
      year: 1971
      finding: 'In M. Greenberger (ed.), Computers, Communications, and the Public Interest (Johns Hopkins Press), pp. 40–41: ''a wealth of information creates a poverty of attention''.'

    axes ax x_label "Miles (thousands)" y_label Price ticks.x [50,60,70] ticks.y false hidden true
    icon car_a at.data [55,3.8] size 80 set twemoji icon_look picture of car hidden true
    text odo_a "59,900" at.data [55,2.3] font_size 30 hidden true
    icon car_b at.data [66,3.8] size 80 set twemoji icon_look picture of car hidden true
    text odo_b "60,100" at.data [66,2.3] font_size 30 hidden true
    text nsales "22 million sales" #8f887c at.data [76,9.4] cites ["lps"] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve price #2f6b8f expr "8.6 - 0.05*(x - 50) - 1.3*floor((x - 50)/10)" x_from 50 x_to 82 hidden true
    dot pa #2f6b8f at.on price at.x 59.9 hidden true
    dot pb #2f6b8f at.on price at.x 60.1 hidden true
    text drop "−$150 to $200" #b5482e at.data [69,8.5] cites ["lps"] font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow drop_arrow #b5482e from.x 65 from.y 8.2 to.x 60.6 to.y 7.4 hidden true
    text ldb "left-digit bias" #b5482e at.data [76,6.9] font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait simon_p cites ["simon"] x 860 y 470 width 200 of "Herbert A. Simon" cameo true hidden true
    text quote "“A wealth of information\ncreates a poverty of attention.”" cites ["simon"] x 430 y 520 font_size 44 hidden true
    text quote_by "Herbert Simon, 1971" #8f887c x 430 y 425 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "The first digit"

Is attention a scarce resource? Start with two used cars, the same in every way except the odometer.
    draw ax

B: One reads fifty-nine thousand nine hundred miles. T (@draw car_b odo_b@)he other, sixty thousand one hundred. Two hundred miles apart.
    draw car_a odo_a

    quiz "Same car, 59,900 vs 60,100 miles. The price gap at auction?" judge false
        intro: Two hundred miles of wear is worth next to nothing. So how far apart are their prices at auction?
        right: Hold on to that guess.
        * Almost nothing
        * About $20
        * About $200

Nicola Lacetera, Devin Pope and Justin Sydnor went through  (@draw nsales@)more than twenty-two million wholesale car sales.
    highlight car_a car_b

B: At every ten-thousand-mile mark, the price falls off a step: about a hundred  (@draw drop drop_arrow@)and fifty to two hundred dollars.
    draw price pa pb

    pause 0.4

Between the marks it slides smoothly. Only a new first digit makes it jump.
    highlight price

    erase nsales

B: Buyers read the first digit and half-ignore the rest. It's called left-digit bias.
    draw ldb

But the full number is right there on the dial. Reading it costs nothing. So aren't people just sloppy?
    highlight odo_a odo_b effect underline

    clear keep []

    card title "The first digit"

B: Herbert Simon had another answer in 1971: a  (@draw quote quote_by@)wealth of information creates a poverty of attention.
    draw simon_p

So reading isn't free after all. Every glance spent on one number is a gl (@erase simon_p@)ance taken from something else.
    text glance "every glance costs" #b5482e x 430 y 330 font_size 40

B: Then attention is a budget, like time or money, and ignoring something may be a choice. To test th (@highlight budget effect underline@)at, we need a price on a glance.
    text budget "attention = a budget" #2f6b8f x 430 y 245 font_size 44

## The price of a glance
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 8.6
    'y':
      - 0
      - 520
    box:
      x: 50
      'y': 150
      w: 740
      h: 500
vars:
    c:
      value: 25
      min: 10
      max: 400
    bx:
      expr: max(0, 20/sqrt(c) - 1)
    bm:
      expr: 400/(1 + bx)
    bt:
      expr: c*bx + bm
    lxa:
      expr: min(6, 430/c) + 0.6
    lya:
      expr: c*min(6, 430/c) - 25
    lxt:
      expr: max(1.0, min(7, 300/c) - 0.5)
    lyt:
      expr: min(c*min(7, 300/c) + 400/(1 + min(7, 300/c)) + 40, 505)
sources:
    - id: sims
      title: Implications of rational inattention
      authors: Sims, C. A.
      year: 2003
      finding: 'Models people as processing information through a channel of limited capacity, so they choose what to learn and respond to news slowly and imperfectly. Journal of Monetary Economics 50(3): 665–690.'
    - id: stigler
      title: The Economics of Information
      authors: Stigler, G. J.
      year: 1961
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 69(3): 213–225. Buyers search among sellers'' prices until the expected saving from one more search equals its cost, so some price dispersion and ignorance persist.'

    axes ax x_label "Hours comparing" y_label "$ a month" ticks.x [0,1,2,3,4,5,6] ticks.y [100,200,300,400,500] hidden true
    curve m #2f6b8f expr "400/(1 + x)" x_from 0 x_to 8 hidden true
    text lab_m "mistake" #2f6b8f at.data [5.6,28] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve a #b5482e expr c*x x_from 0 x_to 8 hidden true
    text lab_a "attention cost" #b5482e at.data [6.6,125] font_size 28 bind {"at.data.0":"lxa","at.data.1":"lya"} hidden true
    curve t #3d3833 expr "c*x + 400/(1 + x)" x_from 0 x_to 8 hidden true
    text lab_t "total" #3d3833 at.data [6.5,265] font_size 28 bind {"at.data.0":"lxt","at.data.1":"lyt"} hidden true
    curve gv dashed #8f887c x_expr bx y_expr t*bm t_from 0 t_to 1 hidden true
    curve gh dashed #8f887c x_expr t*bx y_expr bm t_from 0 t_to 1 hidden true
    dot best #8a5fa8 at.x 3 at.y 100 bind {"at.x":"bx","at.y":"bm"} hidden true
    dot bestt #8a5fa8 at.x 3 at.y 175 bind {"at.x":"bx","at.y":"bt"} hidden true
    icon mia size 70 set twemoji x 890 y 600 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    text mia_t "Mia's flour" x 890 y 540 font_size 28 hidden true
    text readout "look {bx:0} h\nmistake ${bm:0}" x 890 y 440 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ri "rational\ninattention" #2f6b8f cites ["sims"] x 890 y 310 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sims_cue "Stigler 1961\nSims 2003" #8f887c cites ["stigler","sims"] x 890 y 235 font_size 24 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard work [{"tex":"\\text{total} = c\\,h + 400 / (1 + h)"},{"tex":"c = 400 / (1 + h)^2"},{"tex":"h^* = 20 / \\sqrt{c} - 1"}] x 560 y 540 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "The price of a glance"

B: Put a price on a glance and inattention becomes a choice like any other. Take Mia, bu (@draw mia mia_t@)ying flour for her Harbourtown bakery from the mainland.
    draw ax

B: Illustrative numbers: if she never compares suppliers, she overpays about four hundred dollars a month.
    draw m lab_m

And every hour she spends comparing shrinks that mistake.
    highlight m

B: Fast at first, then slower: the first call finds the big savings, the tenth finds pennies.
    blocking false

    point at.data [0.6,250] gesture tap duration 1.5

    point at.data [6,57] gesture tap duration 1.5

But her hour isn't free. Sh (@draw a lab_a@)e could be baking instead. Say it's worth twenty-five dollars.

B: Add the two  (@draw t lab_t@)and you get her total cost. It bottoms out at three hour (@draw gv gh bestt best readout@)s a month, with a hundred dollars of mistake still left.

So the best plan keeps a mistake on purpose?
    highlight best readout

B: Yes. George Stigler priced the search in 1961. Chri (@draw sims_cue@)stopher Sims sharpened it in 2003 into rati (@draw ri@)onal inattention: a mind takes in only so much, so it chooses what to learn.

    quiz "Mia's hour is now worth $100, not $25. What happens?"
        intro: Now the bakery gets busy, and her hour is worth a hundred dollars.
        right: She compares less and errs more: each hour costs four times as much, and saves no more than before.
        wrong: Each hour costs four times as much, and saves no more: here, she compares less and errs more.
        * She compares more and errs less
        + She compares less and errs more
        * Nothing changes

B: Less looking: one hour a month, and the mistake doubles to two hundred dollars.
    animate c 100 duration 2.5 ghost true

Try it: drag the price of her hour, and find where she stops comparing altogether.
    explore params ["c"]

B: From four hundred dollars an hour, she doesn't look at all. Ignoring the information is her best choice.
    animate c 400 duration 2.5

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" intro "Want the sum behind it?" on mathq stage own voice b

@math

    fade m a t lab_m lab_a lab_t gv gh best bestt to 0.2

    erase mathq

Her total is c times her hours, h, plus four hundred over one plus h.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

B: At the bottom, one more hour saves exactly what it costs: c equals four hundred over one plus h, squared.
    draw mcard_line_2

So the best h is twenty over the square root of c, minus one. At twenty-five dollars, three hours; at four hundred, none.
    draw mcard_line_3

    erase mcard

@test

    fade m a t lab_m lab_a lab_t gv gh best bestt to 1

    erase mathq

So ignoring isn't always a lapse. When looking costs more than it saves, not looking is the smart move.
    highlight best readout

## Choice or bias?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: You paid attention exactly where it paid.
    good: A well-spent glance or two.
    poor: Rationally inattentive, perhaps.
    none: Skipping is a choice too. Sims would understand.
sources:
    - id: chetty
      title: 'Salience and Taxation: Theory and Evidence'
      authors: Chetty, R., Looney, A. & Kroft, K.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'Posting tax-inclusive prices on shelf tags in a grocery store reduced demand for the treated products by roughly 8 percent, although the tax itself was unchanged. American Economic Review 99(4): 1145–77.'
    - id: dp
      title: Investor Inattention and Friday Earnings Announcements
      authors: DellaVigna, S. & Pollet, J. M.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'Friday earnings announcements have a 15% lower immediate stock-price response and a 70% higher delayed response than other weekdays. Journal of Finance 64(2): 709–749.'
    - id: lps
      title: Heuristic Thinking and Limited Attention in the Car Market
      authors: Lacetera, N., Pope, D. G. & Sydnor, J. R.
      year: 2012
      finding: 'Used-car auction prices drop discontinuously at 10,000-mile odometer thresholds, roughly $150–$200. American Economic Review 102(5): 2206–36.'

    text clean "look until a glance costs more than it saves" #8f887c x 500 y 628 font_size 30 hidden true
    path line #3d3833 points [[70,470],[930,470]] hidden true
    text end_l "attention optimised" #2f6b8f x 180 y 428 font_size 32 hidden true
    text end_r "bias" #b5482e x 880 y 428 font_size 34 hidden true
    box chip_tax "tax on the tag\nsales −8%" cites ["chetty"] x 190 y 545 width 250 height 90 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    box chip_fri "Friday news\n−15% now, +70% later" cites ["dp"] x 475 y 545 width 290 height 90 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    box chip_odo "odometer\n−$150–200" cites ["lps"] x 800 y 545 width 250 height 90 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    text q_cost "cost of attention = anything?" #b5482e x 500 y 360 font_size 34 hidden true
    mark strike around q_cost kind strike hidden true
    text pinned "cost pinned first → testable" #2f6b8f x 500 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true
    text links "defaults · present bias" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    text conf1 "missed in plain sight: mostly agreed" x 500 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    text conf2 "optimised or bias: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text final "attention: scarce" #2f6b8f x 500 y 330 font_size 48 hidden true

    card title "Choice or bias?"

So a glance has a price, and skipping i (@draw clean@)t can be smart. Do real people behave like Mia?
    draw line end_l end_r

B: Often. When a grocery store printed the sales (@draw chip_tax@) tax into the shelf price instead of adding it at the till, sales of those items fell about eight percent.

And when firms announce earnings on a Friday (@draw chip_fri@), share prices react about fifteen percent less at first, and seventy percent more in the weeks after.

B: Weekend minds are elsewhere. Both fit the model, if noticing takes effort we ration.
    highlight chip_tax chip_fri end_l

The odometer fits worse. The next digit sits right beside the  (@highlight chip_odo end_r@)first, and two hundred dollars ride on it.
    draw chip_odo

B: That's my worry about the label. If any inattention counts as r (@draw q_cost@)ational once you pick the right cost of attention, it explains everything and predicts nothing.

The tautology problem again. Unless the cost  (@draw strike@)is pinned down first: then (@draw pinned@) raise the stakes, and attention must rise. That can fail.

    erase q_cost strike pinned

B: It also help (@draw links@)s a default win: switching means reading the options first, and present bias keeps putting the reading off.

    erase links

How sure are we? That people miss in (@draw conf1@)formation in plain sight, and prices feel it: mostly agreed. Whether th (@draw conf2@)at's optimised or a bias: contested.

    erase conf1 conf2

    quiz "An airline shows a $40 bag fee only at the last checkout step, not in the fare. Compared with putting it in the fare, what should happen?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: More tickets sold: a fee met at the last step gets less attention than one in the headline price.
        wrong: A cost seen late gets less weight, like a tax added at the till: here, more tickets sold.
        + More tickets sold
        * Fewer tickets sold
        * No difference

    quiz "Who should rationally check their savings more often?" voice b
        right: The bigger saver: with more at stake, each glance saves more, so it pays to look more.
        wrong: A bigger possible mistake makes each hour of looking worth more: here, the bigger saver.
        + Someone with $500,000 saved
        * Someone with $5,000 saved
        * Both equally

    quiz "A tax is printed in large type on every shelf tag, yet shoppers still ignore it. Rational inattention can explain this only if…"
        right: Only if reading the tag is costly, which is hard to believe for large print. That's why critics call such rescues too easy.
        wrong: The theory has one dial to turn, the cost of looking: here, reading the tag would have to be costly.
        + Reading the tag is very costly
        * Shoppers love risk
        * The tax is very large

    fade clean to 1

B: So is attention sca (@draw final@)rce? Scarce enough that ignoring can be the best c (@highlight clean@)hoice, and scarce enough to be a cost in every decision.

A hundred lectures in, thanks for spending some of yours here.
    highlight final effect underline

## Where next
